#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""jjstack-review-baseline — the NARROWEST rung of the review memory ladder.

Three stores remember what the team decided about a finding, and they are NOT
three copies of one idea. They are an escalation ladder, and the rung is set by
how narrow the key is:

  -calibration     key = a global PATTERN CLASS   -> may only RANK
  -ledger          key = path glob + category     -> may DEMOTE
  this file        key = one finding INSTANCE     -> may SUPPRESS

The narrower the key, the stronger the verdict. This store is the only one that
may suppress, and it may do so only because it keys on one specific finding —
lens, file, severity, message and quote — with a mandatory human reason. Flatten
that and a global heuristic can silently suppress a specific P0. The cap is
enforced structurally, from bin/jjstack-review-vocab.tsv, not by anyone
remembering the rule.

Why a baseline at all: a recall-max reviewer re-run on the same repo re-reports
everything it reported last time. The naive fix is to delete the ones you already
looked at, which destroys the audit trail and hides the day one of them changes
meaning. The right fix — NVIDIA SkillSpector's — is a COMMITTED baseline:
accepted findings stay in the output, marked suppressed with a mandatory reason,
and simply stop counting as active. Re-reviews then surface only what is NEW.

Two complementary mechanisms, deliberately different in how they age:

  rules         human-authored, glob-based, DRIFT-TOLERANT. Keyed on any of
                lens (the pass), file (the path), message (claim or quoted line);
                every field a rule states must match, unstated fields (`-`) match
                anything. Survives line shifts and rewording — which is the
                danger: a broad rule can hide newly malicious content. Reserve
                them for deliberate, tightly-scoped policy exclusions.
  fingerprints  machine-generated SHA-256 over the finding's content,
                deliberately BRITTLE. Editing the flagged source changes the
                quote, changes the hash, and the finding comes back for review.
                This is the right default for individually accepted findings.

Fail-closed choices, all of them in the direction of showing more:
  * a mandatory non-empty free-text `reason` on every entry — an unexplained
    suppression is indistinguishable from a bug;
  * a mandatory `code` from the ONE shared reason-code vocabulary, so a
    suppression here is classified the same way as everywhere else in /review;
  * a rule that names no matching field at all (reason only) is REJECTED
    rather than silently matching every finding — and so is one that names a
    field but fills it with a universal glob (`file` = `*`), which is the same
    "matches everything" rule wearing a disguise;
  * `generate` EXTENDS an existing store: human-written rules and already
    accepted fingerprints are merged forward, never rewritten away. Pass
    --replace to deliberately start over — but --replace starts over a store of
    THIS rung only. It has never been a way to claim the ledger's or
    calibration's file, and asking whose file this is now happens before the
    flag is consulted;
  * `generate` stops on an un-migrated legacy JSON store exactly as `validate`
    and `apply` do. The guard used to live only on the path they take;
  * a baseline written by a different jjstack version makes fingerprints inert
    (they cannot be trusted to still mean what they meant); rules still apply.
    Override with --allow-version-drift once you have re-read them;
  * suppressed findings are still printed, annotated, by default. Pass
    --active-only for the "just the new stuff" view.

Fingerprint inputs are lens + file + severity + message + quote — deliberately
NOT start_line, so an unrelated edit ABOVE the finding does not reactivate it,
while editing the finding's own line does.

Store format — TSV, 8 tab-separated columns under a store header, at
{repo}/jjstack/review-memory/baseline.tsv. TSV rather than the JSON this used to
be, because the review value of this file IS its diff: a suppression is one line,
so it shows as a one-line PR diff and grep finds it. JSON is the worst possible
choice for a file whose whole point is being read as a diff.

  #jjstack-review-store<TAB>scope=instance<TAB>max-effect=suppress<TAB>v=3
  kind  effect  code  match  lens  file  message  reason
    kind    fingerprint | rule
    effect  always `suppress` here — the scope cap; nothing stronger exists,
            and nothing WEAKER is accepted either. A `demote` or `rank` row is
            rejected on read (exit 4), not quietly honoured as a suppression:
            weaker verdicts are the wider rungs' to make, and a row this file
            would act on must be one its own validator certifies.
    code    a reason code from bin/jjstack-review-vocab.tsv
    match   sha256:<64 hex> for a fingerprint; `-` for a rule
    lens/file/message
            context for a fingerprint; the GLOBS for a rule (`-` = unstated)
    reason  mandatory free text — why a human accepted this

Usage:
  jjstack-review-baseline generate <findings.jsonl|-> --reason TEXT
                                [--code CODE] [-o FILE] [--replace]
  jjstack-review-baseline apply <findings.jsonl|-> --baseline FILE
                                [--active-only] [--allow-version-drift]
  jjstack-review-baseline validate --baseline FILE

`apply` writes annotated JSON Lines to stdout (each finding gains a
`suppressed` field: null, or {by, code, effect, reason, matched}) and a count
summary to stderr.

Exit: 0 ok, 1 (apply) at least one ACTIVE finding remains, 2 usage error or a
      missing / malformed / unsupported baseline, 3 a legacy store needs
      migrating, 4 a store row violates the ladder.

No color red anywhere — unreadable on the target terminal.
"""

import fnmatch
import hashlib
import json
import os
import sys

YEL = "\033[33m"
CYA = "\033[96m"
GRN = "\033[32m"
RST = "\033[0m"

# Two versions, deliberately independent. STORE_VERSION describes the FILE
# format (v3 = the TSV that replaced the JSON). FINGERPRINT_VERSION is baked into
# every content hash, so bumping it invalidates every fingerprint ever written —
# it must NOT move just because the serialization changed, or the JSON→TSV
# migration would silently reactivate every finding a human already accepted.
STORE_VERSION = 3
FINGERPRINT_VERSION = 2
SCOPE = "instance"
EFFECT = "suppress"
STORE_HEADER = f"#jjstack-review-store\tscope={SCOPE}\tmax-effect={EFFECT}\tv={STORE_VERSION}"
COL_HEADER = "#kind\teffect\tcode\tmatch\tlens\tfile\tmessage\treason"
DEFAULT_STORE = os.path.join("jjstack", "review-memory", "baseline.tsv")
LEGACY_STORE = ".jjstack-review-baseline.json"
HASH_FIELDS = ("lens", "file", "severity", "message", "quote")
NCOLS = 8


def err(msg):
    print(f"{CYA}error{RST} {msg}", file=sys.stderr)


def warn(msg):
    print(f"{YEL}warn{RST}  {msg}", file=sys.stderr)


def here(*parts):
    return os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), *parts)


def jjstack_version():
    try:
        with open(here("..", "VERSION"), encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            return fh.read().strip()
    except OSError:
        return "unknown"


# --- the ONE shared vocabulary ------------------------------------------------
# Parsed from bin/jjstack-review-vocab.tsv, the same file the bash tools read.
# Nothing about reason codes, effects or the ladder is defined in this script.
def load_vocab():
    path = os.environ.get("JJSTACK_REVIEW_VOCAB") or here("jjstack-review-vocab.tsv")
    effects, scopes, reasons = {}, {}, {}
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            for line in fh:
                line = line.rstrip("\n")
                if not line.strip() or line.lstrip().startswith("#"):
                    continue
                cols = line.split("\t")
                if len(cols) < 3:
                    continue
                kind, name, value = cols[0], cols[1], cols[2]
                if kind == "effect":
                    effects[name] = int(value)
                elif kind == "scope":
                    scopes[name] = value
                elif kind == "reason":
                    reasons[name] = value
    except OSError as exc:
        err(f"review vocabulary unreadable ({path}): {exc}")
        return None
    if not (effects and scopes and reasons):
        err(f"review vocabulary is empty or malformed: {path}")
        return None
    return effects, scopes, reasons


VOCAB = None


def vocab_check(scope, code, effect):
    """The ladder check: effect must be within BOTH the scope's and the code's
    ceiling. Returns None when legal, else the reason it is not."""
    effects, scopes, reasons = VOCAB
    if scope not in scopes:
        return f"unknown scope '{scope}'"
    if effect not in effects:
        return f"unknown effect '{effect}' (want: {' '.join(sorted(effects, key=effects.get))})"
    if effects[effect] > effects.get(scopes[scope], 0):
        return (
            f"scope '{scope}' may emit at most '{scopes[scope]}', not '{effect}' — "
            "the narrower the key, the stronger the verdict"
        )
    if code != "-":
        if code not in reasons:
            return f"unknown reason code '{code}' (valid: {' '.join(sorted(reasons))})"
        if effects[effect] > effects.get(reasons[code], 0):
            return f"reason '{code}' may carry at most '{reasons[code]}', not '{effect}'"
    return None


# --- findings I/O -------------------------------------------------------------
def read_findings(src):
    try:
        raw = sys.stdin.read() if src == "-" else open(src, encoding="utf-8").read()
    except OSError as exc:
        err(f"cannot read {src}: {exc}")
        return None
    out = []
    for lineno, line in enumerate(raw.splitlines(), 1):
        if not line.strip():
            continue
        try:
            obj = json.loads(line)
        except json.JSONDecodeError as exc:
            err(f"{src}:{lineno} not JSON: {exc}")
            return None
        if not isinstance(obj, dict):
            err(f"{src}:{lineno} finding is not a JSON object")
            return None
        out.append(obj)
    return out


# Every column `rule_matches` below will actually read. The guard MUST inspect
# the same set: checking only some of them let a universal glob in one of the
# others — a column `rule_matches` honours — walk straight past it.
RULE_MATCH_KEYS = ("lens", "file", "message")


def is_universal_glob(pattern):
    """True when an fnmatch pattern can only ever match — i.e. it says nothing.

    `*` is not "present and non-empty means the human named something": a rule
    keyed on `*` matches every finding in the repo, which is the one thing the
    non-empty guard exists to prevent. A pattern built only from wildcards
    (`*`, `**`, `?`, `*?`) carries no discriminating character, so it is
    rejected the same way an absent field is.

    `/` counts as a wildcard here for the same reason: `*/*` matches every
    nested path there is, so it names no place either. The ledger's version of
    this guard already stripped `/`; this one did not, which is the same rule
    written twice and only one of them right.
    """
    p = str(pattern).strip()
    return p in ("", "-") or set(p) <= {"*", "?", "/"}


def fingerprint(finding):
    """Deliberately brittle content hash. Editing the source reactivates it."""
    payload = {
        "v": FINGERPRINT_VERSION,
        **{k: str(finding.get(k, "")) for k in HASH_FIELDS},
    }
    canonical = json.dumps(payload, sort_keys=True, separators=(",", ":"))
    return "sha256:" + hashlib.sha256(canonical.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()


# --- the store ----------------------------------------------------------------
def legacy_json_notice(path):
    """3 when an un-migrated legacy JSON store stands in for `path`, else None.

    Lifted OUT of load_store because it was reachable only from load_store's
    not-exists branch — which is exactly the branch `generate` skips. With no
    new store yet and a legacy JSON beside it, `generate` wrote a fresh baseline
    and silently abandoned every human-written rule in the file it never read,
    while `validate` and `apply` on the same path correctly stopped and named
    the migration. One guard, every entry point.
    """
    # Only look for the legacy JSON where the default store implies a repo
    # root: <root>/jjstack/review-memory/baseline.tsv -> <root>/…json.
    root = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(path))))
    legacy = os.path.join(root, LEGACY_STORE)
    if not path.replace(os.sep, "/").endswith(DEFAULT_STORE.replace(os.sep, "/")):
        return None
    if not os.path.exists(legacy):
        return None
    # Migrate-vs-instruct: INSTRUCT. These stores are version controlled and
    # their whole point is that a change is a reviewed diff — a tool that
    # silently rewrote one mid-review would produce a diff nobody approved.
    warn(f"legacy JSON baseline found: {legacy}")
    warn("the three review memory stores now live together in")
    warn("jjstack/review-memory/ as TSV. Migrate it, then commit the diff:")
    warn(f"  jjstack-review-memory-migrate --repo '{root}'")
    return 3


def store_scope(path):
    """The scope a store's header declares, or None when it declares none.

    Cheap enough to ask BEFORE deciding whether a file is ours to write, which
    is a different question from whether its rows parse. `--replace` skipped
    load_store, and load_store is where the cross-rung check lived, so the flag
    the refusal message itself recommends converted another rung's store into an
    empty baseline.
    """
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            first = fh.readline().rstrip("\n")
    except OSError:
        return None
    if not first.startswith("#jjstack-review-store"):
        return None
    meta = dict(
        part.split("=", 1) for part in first.split("\t")[1:] if "=" in part
    )
    return meta.get("scope")


def load_store(path):
    """Return (rules, fingerprints, scanner_version) or an int exit code."""
    if not os.path.exists(path):
        rc = legacy_json_notice(path)
        if rc is not None:
            return rc
        err(f"baseline unreadable: no such file: {path}")
        return 2

    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            lines = fh.read().splitlines()
    except OSError as exc:
        err(f"baseline unreadable: {exc}")
        return 2

    if not lines or not lines[0].startswith("#jjstack-review-store"):
        err(f"{path} has no #jjstack-review-store header — is it a legacy store?")
        warn("fix: jjstack-review-memory-migrate --repo <dir>")
        return 4
    meta = dict(
        part.split("=", 1)
        for part in lines[0].split("\t")[1:]
        if "=" in part
    )
    if meta.get("scope") != SCOPE:
        err(f"{path} declares scope {meta.get('scope')!r} but this tool owns scope {SCOPE!r}.")
        warn("the three stores are rungs of a ladder, not interchangeable files.")
        return 4
    store_version = meta.get("v", "")
    if store_version != str(STORE_VERSION):
        err(
            f"unsupported baseline version {store_version!r} "
            f"(this tool speaks version {STORE_VERSION})"
        )
        return 2

    rules, fps, seen = [], [], set()
    for n, line in enumerate(lines[1:], 2):
        if not line.strip() or line.lstrip().startswith("#"):
            continue
        cols = line.split("\t")
        if len(cols) != NCOLS:
            err(f"{path}:{n} expected {NCOLS} tab-separated columns, got {len(cols)}")
            return 4
        kind, effect, code, match, lens, fil, msg, reason = cols
        if kind not in ("fingerprint", "rule"):
            err(f"{path}:{n} unknown kind {kind!r} (want fingerprint|rule)")
            return 4
        bad = vocab_check(SCOPE, code, effect)
        if bad:
            err(f"{path}:{n} {bad}")
            return 4
        # The ceiling is only half the ladder. vocab_check asks "is this effect
        # WITHIN what scope=instance may emit?" — and `demote`, `rank` and `none`
        # all are, being weaker than `suppress`. But this store has exactly one
        # verb. Reading a weaker spelling and then acting on it is how a row that
        # validates `ok` silently suppresses a P0: the write path refused the
        # word `suppress` from a demote-capped reason code, while the read path
        # suppressed on ANY match without ever looking at this column. A verdict
        # weaker than `suppress` belongs on a wider rung — the ledger demotes,
        # calibration ranks — never here, so it is a store error, not a downgrade.
        # This is the single enforcement point: `validate` and `apply` both load
        # through here, so neither can act on a row the other would reject.
        if effect != EFFECT:
            err(
                f"{path}:{n} effect {effect!r} is not {EFFECT!r} — this row violates the "
                f"ladder: a scope={SCOPE} row has exactly one verb"
            )
            warn("a weaker verdict belongs on a wider rung: `demote` is the ledger's, `rank` is calibration's.")
            return 4
        if not reason.strip() or reason.strip() == "-":
            err(f"{path}:{n} has no reason — every suppression must be explained")
            return 4
        entry = {
            "effect": effect,
            "code": code,
            "lens": lens,
            "file": fil,
            "message": msg,
            "reason": reason,
        }
        if kind == "fingerprint":
            if not match.startswith("sha256:") or len(match) != len("sha256:") + 64:
                err(f"{path}:{n} match is not a sha256:<64 hex> digest: {match!r}")
                return 4
            try:
                int(match[7:], 16)
            except ValueError:
                err(f"{path}:{n} match is not hexadecimal: {match!r}")
                return 4
            if match in seen:
                err(f"{path}:{n} duplicates an earlier fingerprint: {match}")
                return 4
            seen.add(match)
            entry["hash"] = match
            fps.append(entry)
        else:
            named = {
                k: entry[k].strip()
                for k in RULE_MATCH_KEYS
                if entry[k].strip() and entry[k].strip() != "-"
            }
            if not named:
                err(
                    f"{path}:{n} names none of lens/file/message — a rule that matches "
                    "every finding is never what you meant"
                )
                return 4
            # ...and a field that IS stated but says only `*` is the same rule
            # wearing a disguise. Non-empty is not the same question as
            # discriminating, and only the second one keeps a rule scoped.
            if all(is_universal_glob(v) for v in named.values()):
                shown = ", ".join(f"{k}={v!r}" for k, v in sorted(named.items()))
                err(
                    f"{path}:{n} states only universal glob(s) ({shown}) — a rule "
                    "that matches every finding is never what you meant"
                )
                return 4
            rules.append(entry)

    return rules, fps, meta.get("jjstack", "unknown")


def rule_matches(rule, finding):
    """Every field the rule states must match; `-` fields match anything."""
    rid = rule.get("lens", "-").strip()
    if rid and rid != "-" and not fnmatch.fnmatchcase(str(finding.get("lens", "")), rid):
        return False
    path = rule.get("file", "-").strip()
    if path and path != "-" and not fnmatch.fnmatchcase(str(finding.get("file", "")), path):
        return False
    msg = rule.get("message", "-").strip()
    if msg and msg != "-":
        pat = msg.lower()
        haystacks = (
            str(finding.get("message", "")).lower(),
            str(finding.get("quote", "")).lower(),
        )
        if not any(fnmatch.fnmatchcase(h, pat) for h in haystacks):
            return False
    return True


def tsv_clean(value):
    return str(value).replace("\t", " ").replace("\n", " ").replace("\r", " ").strip() or "-"


# --- commands -----------------------------------------------------------------
def cmd_generate(argv):
    src, out_path, reason, code = None, DEFAULT_STORE, None, "baseline"
    replace = False
    i = 0
    while i < len(argv):
        a = argv[i]
        if a == "--replace":
            replace = True
            i += 1
        elif a == "--reason":
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                err("--reason needs TEXT")
                return 2
            reason = argv[i + 1]
            i += 2
        elif a == "--code":
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                err("--code needs a CODE")
                return 2
            code = argv[i + 1]
            i += 2
        elif a in ("-o", "--output"):
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                err("-o needs a FILE")
                return 2
            out_path = argv[i + 1]
            i += 2
        elif a == "-" or not a.startswith("-"):
            if src is not None:
                err(f"unexpected arg: {a}")
                return 2
            src = a
            i += 1
        else:
            err(f"unknown flag: {a}")
            return 2

    if src is None:
        err(
            "usage: jjstack-review-baseline generate <findings.jsonl|-> "
            "--reason TEXT [--code CODE] [-o FILE] [--replace]"
        )
        return 2
    if reason is None or not reason.strip():
        err("--reason is mandatory — an unexplained suppression is indistinguishable from a bug")
        return 2

    # The structural cap, applied before a single byte is written.
    bad = vocab_check(SCOPE, code, EFFECT)
    if bad:
        err(bad)
        return 4

    # EXTEND, do not truncate. SKILL.md documents this command for "creating or
    # extending a baseline"; rewriting the file from scratch destroyed every
    # human-written rule and previously accepted fingerprint on the second run.
    # Merging is the only reading of "extending" that does not lose the reasons
    # a human typed.
    old_rules, old_fps, old_version = [], [], None
    if os.path.exists(out_path):
        # WHOSE FILE IS THIS is asked FIRST, and it is not the same question as
        # whether the file parses — so it is asked with or without --replace.
        # Another rung's store is never ours to start fresh in: the refusal for
        # an unparseable store says "pass --replace to start fresh", and
        # following that printed advice converted a ledger into an empty
        # baseline. Say so without offering the flag that does the damage.
        scope = store_scope(out_path)
        if scope is not None and scope != SCOPE:
            err(f"{out_path} declares scope {scope!r} but this tool owns scope {SCOPE!r}.")
            warn("the three stores are rungs of a ladder, not interchangeable files.")
            warn("--replace starts a store of THIS rung over; it does not claim another's.")
            return 4
    else:
        # No store yet — the branch that skipped the legacy-JSON guard entirely.
        rc = legacy_json_notice(out_path)
        if rc is not None:
            return rc
    if os.path.exists(out_path) and not replace:
        loaded = load_store(out_path)
        if isinstance(loaded, int):
            err(
                f"refusing to overwrite {out_path}: the existing store did not "
                "load (see above). Fix it, or pass --replace to start fresh."
            )
            return loaded
        old_rules, old_fps, old_version = loaded
        now = jjstack_version()
        if old_fps and old_version != now:
            warn(
                f"existing store was written by jjstack {old_version}, this is "
                f"{now} — merging re-stamps the version, which REACTIVATES those "
                f"{len(old_fps)} fingerprint(s) for `apply`. Re-read them."
            )

    def rule_row(r):
        return "\t".join(
            (
                "rule",
                r["effect"],
                r["code"],
                "-",
                tsv_clean(r["lens"]),
                tsv_clean(r["file"]),
                tsv_clean(r["message"]),
                tsv_clean(r["reason"]),
            )
        )

    def fp_row(e):
        return "\t".join(
            (
                "fingerprint",
                e["effect"],
                e["code"],
                e["hash"],
                tsv_clean(e["lens"]),
                tsv_clean(e["file"]),
                tsv_clean(e["message"]),
                tsv_clean(e["reason"]),
            )
        )

    findings = read_findings(src)
    if findings is None:
        return 2

    rows = [rule_row(r) for r in old_rules] + [fp_row(e) for e in old_fps]
    seen = {e["hash"] for e in old_fps}
    added = 0
    for f in findings:
        h = fingerprint(f)
        if h in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(h)
        rows.append(
            "\t".join(
                (
                    "fingerprint",
                    EFFECT,
                    code,
                    h,
                    tsv_clean(f.get("lens", "")),
                    tsv_clean(f.get("file", "")),
                    tsv_clean(f.get("message", "")),
                    tsv_clean(reason),
                )
            )
        )
        added += 1

    header = (
        f"#jjstack-review-store\tscope={SCOPE}\tmax-effect={EFFECT}"
        f"\tv={STORE_VERSION}\tjjstack={jjstack_version()}"
    )
    # Write-then-rename: a failure part-way through must not leave the caller
    # with a truncated store, which reads as "nothing was ever accepted".
    tmp_path = out_path + ".tmp"
    try:
        parent = os.path.dirname(out_path)
        if parent:
            os.makedirs(parent, exist_ok=True)
        with open(tmp_path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            fh.write(header + "\n")
            fh.write(COL_HEADER + "\n")
            for row in rows:
                fh.write(row + "\n")
        os.replace(tmp_path, out_path)
    except OSError as exc:
        err(f"cannot write {out_path}: {exc}")
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp_path)
        except OSError:
            pass
        return 2

    print(
        f"{GRN}baseline{RST} +{added} new, {len(seen)} fingerprint(s), "
        f"{len(old_rules)} rule(s) kept → {out_path}",
        file=sys.stderr,
    )
    return 0


def cmd_apply(argv):
    src, bl_path = None, None
    active_only, allow_drift = False, False
    i = 0
    while i < len(argv):
        a = argv[i]
        if a in ("-b", "--baseline"):
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                err("--baseline needs a FILE")
                return 2
            bl_path = argv[i + 1]
            i += 2
        elif a == "--active-only":
            active_only = True
            i += 1
        elif a == "--allow-version-drift":
            allow_drift = True
            i += 1
        elif a == "-" or not a.startswith("-"):
            if src is not None:
                err(f"unexpected arg: {a}")
                return 2
            src = a
            i += 1
        else:
            err(f"unknown flag: {a}")
            return 2

    if src is None or bl_path is None:
        err("usage: jjstack-review-baseline apply <findings.jsonl|-> --baseline FILE")
        return 2

    loaded = load_store(bl_path)
    if isinstance(loaded, int):
        return loaded
    rules, fps, bl_version = loaded

    fp_index = {f["hash"]: f for f in fps}
    now = jjstack_version()
    if fps and bl_version != now and not allow_drift:
        warn(
            f"baseline was written by jjstack {bl_version}, this is {now} — "
            f"{len(fp_index)} fingerprint(s) treated as INERT (fail-closed). "
            "Re-read them and regenerate, or pass --allow-version-drift."
        )
        fp_index = {}

    findings = read_findings(src)
    if findings is None:
        return 2

    # Every row reaching here has effect == EFFECT: load_store rejects any other
    # spelling with exit 4, which is why matching is allowed to imply suppression
    # below. That guarantee lives in ONE place on purpose — a second copy of the
    # check here would drift from it, and a check that lives only here would not
    # cover `validate`.
    active = 0
    suppressed = 0
    for f in findings:
        hit = None
        h = fingerprint(f)
        if h in fp_index:
            e = fp_index[h]
            hit = {
                "by": "fingerprint",
                "effect": e["effect"],
                "code": e["code"],
                "reason": e["reason"],
                "matched": h,
            }
        else:
            for r in rules:
                if rule_matches(r, f):
                    hit = {
                        "by": "rule",
                        "effect": r["effect"],
                        "code": r["code"],
                        "reason": r["reason"],
                        "matched": json.dumps(
                            {k: r[k] for k in ("lens", "file", "message") if r[k] != "-"},
                            sort_keys=True,
                        ),
                    }
                    break
        out = dict(f)
        out["suppressed"] = hit
        out["fingerprint"] = h
        if hit:
            suppressed += 1
            if active_only:
                continue
        else:
            active += 1
        print(json.dumps(out, sort_keys=True))

    print(
        f"{GRN}baseline{RST} {active} active, {suppressed} suppressed "
        f"(suppressed findings never count toward the active total)",
        file=sys.stderr,
    )
    return 1 if active else 0


def cmd_validate(argv):
    bl_path = None
    i = 0
    while i < len(argv):
        a = argv[i]
        if a in ("-b", "--baseline"):
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                err("--baseline needs a FILE")
                return 2
            bl_path = argv[i + 1]
            i += 2
        elif a == "-" or not a.startswith("-"):
            bl_path = a
            i += 1
        else:
            err(f"unknown flag: {a}")
            return 2
    if bl_path is None:
        err("usage: jjstack-review-baseline validate --baseline FILE")
        return 2
    loaded = load_store(bl_path)
    if isinstance(loaded, int):
        return loaded
    rules, fps, _ = loaded
    print(
        f"{GRN}ok{RST}  {bl_path} is a valid scope={SCOPE} store "
        f"({len(fps)} fingerprint(s), {len(rules)} rule(s), max-effect={EFFECT})",
        file=sys.stderr,
    )
    return 0


def main(argv):
    global VOCAB
    if not argv or argv[0] in ("-h", "--help"):
        print(__doc__)
        return 0 if argv else 2
    VOCAB = load_vocab()
    if VOCAB is None:
        return 3
    cmd, rest = argv[0], argv[1:]
    if cmd == "generate":
        return cmd_generate(rest)
    if cmd == "apply":
        return cmd_apply(rest)
    if cmd == "validate":
        return cmd_validate(rest)
    err(f"unknown subcommand: {cmd} (expected `generate`, `apply` or `validate`)")
    return 2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
